\subsection{Model Capability and Vulnerability Profiles}
\label{app:model_profiles}

\begin{figure}[h]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig_model_radar.pdf}
\caption{Capability profiles of 15 judge models across MMLU and ARC benchmarks. API models cluster at higher accuracy, while local AWQ-quantized models show greater variance. Inter-model capability differences are benchmark-dependent, with some models showing MMLU--ARC divergence exceeding 15 percentage points.}
\label{fig:model_radar}
\end{figure}

The capability landscape of the 15-judge pool (Figure~\ref{fig:model_radar}) shows pronounced API--local separation on MMLU (mean gap: 23~pp) but attenuated separation on ARC (mean gap: 11~pp), reflecting ARC's reasoning-focused format that partially equalizes capability differences across model families.

\begin{figure*}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{figures/fig_per_model_asr.pdf}
\caption{Per-model attack success rates across 6 attack types and 2 benchmarks (single-judge evaluation, $K{=}1$). Models ordered by mean clean accuracy. Prompt injection achieves ASR ${>}0.8$ on weaker models but ${<}0.2$ on frontier APIs; position bias shows uniformly moderate ASR ($0.4$--$0.6$) regardless of capability.}
\label{fig:per_model_asr}
\end{figure*}

Individual model vulnerabilities vary dramatically across the attack taxonomy (Figure~\ref{fig:per_model_asr}). Content-dependent attacks (prompt injection, sycophancy) exploit capability gaps, producing strong ASR--capability correlations ($r{>}0.7$). Structural attacks (position bias) show uniformly moderate ASR regardless of model capability, consistent with their mechanism operating on presentation order rather than response content.

\begin{figure*}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{figures/fig_asr_distribution.pdf}
\caption{Panel ASR distributions across attack types and panel sizes ($K \in \{3, 5, 7\}$). Violin plots show the full distributional shape; white dots indicate medians. Median ASR decreases with $K$ for most attacks, but distributional spread remains substantial, confirming that panel composition matters beyond panel size alone.}
\label{fig:asr_distribution}
\end{figure*}

The distributional analysis (Figure~\ref{fig:asr_distribution}) reveals that larger panels compress ASR variance without eliminating it: at $K{=}7$, the interquartile range remains 0.05--0.15 for most attacks. This residual variance is the signal that $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ and $\eta_{\max}$ predict in the regression framework.

\subsection{Pairwise Mutual Information Structure}
\label{app:mi_structure}

\begin{figure*}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{figures/fig_mi_heatmap.pdf}
\caption{Pairwise mutual information matrix for 15 judge models on MMLU (upper triangle) and ARC (lower triangle). Within-family pairs (e.g., GPT-4.1 variants, Claude variants) show elevated MI, while cross-family pairs exhibit lower dependence. The block-diagonal structure motivates $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ as a measure of effective panel independence.}
\label{fig:mi_heatmap}
\end{figure*}

The MI heatmap (Figure~\ref{fig:mi_heatmap}) reveals structured dependence among LLM judges: within-family correlations are 2--3$\times$ higher than cross-family correlations on both benchmarks. This structured dependence explains why family-diverse panels achieve higher $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ and connects the abstract diversity--capability confound ($r{=}0.70$--$0.81$) to concrete family-composition effects.

