\section{Panel Composition Analysis}
\label{app:panel_composition}

\begin{figure*}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{figures/fig_panel_composition.pdf}
\caption{API model fraction vs.\ panel ASR heatmap across attack types ($K{=}5$, $n{=}3{,}003$ panels). Each cell shows the mean ASR for panels with a given API model fraction. All-API panels (fraction${=}1.0$) achieve the lowest ASR for most attacks, but mixed-composition panels show heterogeneous vulnerability patterns not captured by API fraction alone.}
\label{fig:panel_composition}
\end{figure*}

The composition heatmap (Figure~\ref{fig:panel_composition}) connects the abstract $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$--ASR relationship to concrete panel design choices. The gradient from high-API to low-API panels reveals that capability concentration (all API models) does not uniformly minimize vulnerability: for position bias, all-API panels show ASR comparable to mixed panels, consistent with the structural nature of this attack. The heatmap also illustrates why the diversity--capability confound is difficult to break: high-API panels simultaneously achieve low $\eta_{\max}$ and low $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$, conflating the two predictors.

\section{Regression Coefficient Trajectories}
\label{app:beta_trajectory}

\begin{figure}[h]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig_beta_trajectory.pdf}
\caption{Standardized regression coefficient ($\hat{\beta}_{\mathrm{std}}$) trajectories with 95\% CI bands across panel sizes ($K \in \{3, 5, 7\}$) from meta-analytic pooling (DerSimonian--Laird). $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ (blue) maintains positive coefficients across all $K$; $\eta_{\max}$ (orange) declines monotonically, with the CI crossing zero at $K{\geq}5$.}
\label{fig:beta_trajectory}
\end{figure}

The trajectory plot (Figure~\ref{fig:beta_trajectory}) provides a visual summary of the paper's central finding: the diminishing weakest link. The $\eta_{\max}$ pooled coefficient shrinks from $\hat{\beta}_{\mathrm{std}}{=}0.264$ [$0.158, 0.369$] at $K{=}3$ to $0.084$ [$-0.083, 0.251$] at $K{=}7$, while $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ remains stable ($0.356$--$0.436$). The $K^*$ crossover between the two predictors occurs in the interval $(3, 5)$, coinciding with the power threshold where $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$'s MDE drops below the observed effect size (Table~\ref{tab:mde}).

